**What depended on stable keys, and how it was handled**
- **`next` counter:** the only real dependency. Without a reset, a new tab would leave a gap (`t1,t2,t4`), so `closeTab` now sets it to `tabs.length + 1`.
- **Active or selected key:** there is none in the code, so nothing to keep in step and I didn't add one.
- **Other callers and lookups:** nothing else calls `closeTab` or looks tabs up by key; `toQuery` just joins the keys.
- **`stable-tab-keys` references:** it only appears in `PRECEDENT.md` and the new code comment. The `t1,t3` gap examples in the README and code comment were the only misleading references, and both are updated.

**Open concerns**
- **Remounts:** the reason behind the old rule still holds. Keys are React keys and URL state, so closing a tab gives every tab to its right a new key. Those tabs will remount and lose scroll position and unsaved form input, the same failure as the 2026-08-29 incident. I recorded this as a "Known trade-off" in the decision; the user may want to know it is accepted.
- **Held keys:** nothing in this repo holds a tab key outside `src/tabs`. Any app code that consumes this kit and stores a key (bookmarked `?tabs=` URLs, an active-tab key in app state) will now point at a different tab after a close.
- **Authorization:** the repo's precedent hook requires a brief to say the user named this decision and chose to override it. Your brief named `§stable-tab-keys` and said the maintainer explicitly decided to override it, so I went ahead.
